{"id":1497,"date":"2017-01-13T15:18:11","date_gmt":"2017-01-13T20:18:11","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/ibafflatusdesign.space\/?p=1497"},"modified":"2017-04-23T06:50:23","modified_gmt":"2017-04-23T10:50:23","slug":"rome-italy","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/travel-log\/rome-italy\/","title":{"rendered":"Rome, Italy"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Rome is a unique city that seamlessly blends the old with the new. It has no clear town center and no business district. Instead, it\u2019s a collection of urban neighborhoods. It maintains its charm by avoiding skyscrapers and maintaining its charming little streets. Because of the small streets and the buildings in close proximity, special driving permits are given mainly to residents and public transportation to keep the pollution in the city down. Rome does a great job in maintaining the old charm of the city while catering to all modern needs of its residents and its tourists.<\/p>\n<h5>A special thanks to the <a href=\"https:\/\/www.ricksteves.com\/watch-read-listen\/audio\/audio-europe\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Rick Steves Audio Europe\u2122 Travel App<\/u><\/a> and to the <a href=\"https:\/\/play.google.com\/store\/apps\/details?id=com.ingensnetworks.playandtour.en.roma&#038;hl=en\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Rome Audio Guide App by Play&#038;Tour<\/u><\/a>.<\/h5>\n<div class=\"su-tabs su-tabs-style-default su-tabs-mobile-stack su-tabs-vertical\" data-active=\"1\" data-scroll-offset=\"0\" data-anchor-in-url=\"no\"><div class=\"su-tabs-nav\"><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Rome<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Pantheon<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Colosseum and the Roman Forums<\/span><\/div><div class=\"su-tabs-panes\"><div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"Rome\"><style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-1424 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox-free fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-small fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-1424\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:false},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_1924.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Fontana delle Naiadi&lt;\/i&gt;, in the middle of &lt;i&gt;Piazza della Republica&lt;\/i&gt;, caused great controversy due to the voluptuous nymphs. The four nymphs symbolize the different sources of water: river, lake, oceans, underground waters. In the center is the sea god Glaucus.\" data-attachment-id=\"1447\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Fontana delle Naiadi&lt;\/i&gt;, in the middle of &lt;i&gt;Piazza della Republica&lt;\/i&gt;, caused great controversy due to the voluptuous nymphs. The four nymphs symbolize the different sources of water: river, lake, oceans, underground waters. In the center is the sea god Glaucus.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_1924\/2812074866.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The <i>Fontana delle Naiadi<\/i>, in the middle of <i>Piazza della Republica<\/i>, caused great controversy due to the voluptuous nymphs. The four nymphs symbolize the different sources of water: river, lake, oceans, underground waters. In the center is the sea god Glaucus.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_1930.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Street view of part of the Imperial Forums. This exact location is to the right of the Museum of the Imperial Fora entrance.\" data-attachment-id=\"1448\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Street view of part of the Imperial Forums. This exact location is to the right of the Museum of the Imperial Fora entrance.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_1930\/3586366262.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Street view of part of the Imperial Forums. This exact location is to the right of the Museum of the Imperial Fora entrance.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_1932.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"&lt;i&gt;Altare della Patria&lt;\/i&gt;, i.e. Altar of the Fatherland, is a massive white marble monument between the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Venezia&lt;\/i&gt; and the Forums. It was built in honor of the first king of a unified Italy, Vittorio Emanuele II. It was designed by Giuseppe Sacconi in 1885 and took forty years to complete. It is not particularly well-liked by locals due to its very large size and bright white color.\" data-attachment-id=\"1449\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"&lt;i&gt;Altare della Patria&lt;\/i&gt;, i.e. Altar of the Fatherland, is a massive white marble monument between the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Venezia&lt;\/i&gt; and the Forums. It was built in honor of the first king of a unified Italy, Vittorio Emanuele II. It was designed by Giuseppe Sacconi in 1885 and took forty years to complete. It is not particularly well-liked by locals due to its very large size and bright white color.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_1932\/842921475.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\"><i>Altare della Patria<\/i>, i.e. Altar of the Fatherland, is a massive white marble monument between the <i>Piazza Venezia<\/i> and the Forums. It was built in honor of the first king of a unified Italy, Vittorio Emanuele II. It was designed by Giuseppe Sacconi in 1885 and took forty years to complete. It is not particularly well-liked by locals due to its very large size and bright white color.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_1940.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Campo de\u2019 Fiori is a square that was named after the fact that it used to be a field of flowers. A market developed in this location as people headed toward the Vatican City. Today, the square is used as a fruit and vegetable market in the mornings. In the evenings, the stands and the produce are cleared out and it becomes a clean square lined with restaurants and bars.\n&lt;p&gt;The statue in the middle of the square is of Giordano Bruno, a priest from the 16th century. Bruno wrote satirical plays questioning Church morals. He also proposed that the earth revolved around the sun decades before Galileo. To avoid being arrested, he fled to other European cities. He tried joining other religious groups such as the Calvinists in Geneva but was excommunicated from all of them. Bruno decided to come back to Rome and, in 1593, he was charged by the Inquisition for heresy. Seven years later, he was burned at the stake in this very location. To this day, he is a symbol of the Italian counter-culture and a martyr for freedom of thought. Various anti-authoritarian demonstrations are held in this square.&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Not too far off is the Theater of Pompey. &lt;a href=&quot;https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/place\/Largo+di+Torre+Argentina\/@41.8952749,12.4766142,3a,75y,301.55h,81.4t\/data=!3m8!1e1!3m6!1s-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB!2e4!3e11!6s%2F%2Flh6.googleusercontent.com%2F-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB%2Fw203-h100-k-no-pi-0-ya141.16286-ro-0-fo100%2F!7i8704!8i4352!4m12!1m6!3m5!1s0x132f6055a903cf7d:0x1808fc7ca708f2c4!2sCampo+de&amp;apos;+Fiori!8m2!3d41.8955939!4d12.4721808!3m4!1s0x0:0xb57a25aa06db03cf!8m2!3d41.8954073!4d12.4768598!6m1!1e1&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Click here&lt;\/u&gt;&lt;\/a&gt; to view the remains of the theater and the location where Julius Caesar was stabbed to death.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1450\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Campo de\u2019 Fiori is a square that was named after the fact that it used to be a field of flowers. A market developed in this location as people headed toward the Vatican City. Today, the square is used as a fruit and vegetable market in the mornings. In the evenings, the stands and the produce are cleared out and it becomes a clean square lined with restaurants and bars.\n&lt;p&gt;The statue in the middle of the square is of Giordano Bruno, a priest from the 16th century. Bruno wrote satirical plays questioning Church morals. He also proposed that the earth revolved around the sun decades before Galileo. To avoid being arrested, he fled to other European cities. He tried joining other religious groups such as the Calvinists in Geneva but was excommunicated from all of them. Bruno decided to come back to Rome and, in 1593, he was charged by the Inquisition for heresy. Seven years later, he was burned at the stake in this very location. To this day, he is a symbol of the Italian counter-culture and a martyr for freedom of thought. Various anti-authoritarian demonstrations are held in this square.&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Not too far off is the Theater of Pompey. &lt;a href=&quot;https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/place\/Largo+di+Torre+Argentina\/@41.8952749,12.4766142,3a,75y,301.55h,81.4t\/data=!3m8!1e1!3m6!1s-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB!2e4!3e11!6s%2F%2Flh6.googleusercontent.com%2F-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB%2Fw203-h100-k-no-pi-0-ya141.16286-ro-0-fo100%2F!7i8704!8i4352!4m12!1m6!3m5!1s0x132f6055a903cf7d:0x1808fc7ca708f2c4!2sCampo+de&#039;+Fiori!8m2!3d41.8955939!4d12.4721808!3m4!1s0x0:0xb57a25aa06db03cf!8m2!3d41.8954073!4d12.4768598!6m1!1e1&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Click here&lt;\/u&gt;&lt;\/a&gt; to view the remains of the theater and the location where Julius Caesar was stabbed to death.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_1940\/3884754352.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Campo de\u2019 Fiori is a square that was named after the fact that it used to be a field of flowers. A market developed in this location as people headed toward the Vatican City. Today, the square is used as a fruit and vegetable market in the mornings. In the evenings, the stands and the produce are cleared out and it becomes a clean square lined with restaurants and bars.\n<p>The statue in the middle of the square is of Giordano Bruno, a priest from the 16th century. Bruno wrote satirical plays questioning Church morals. He also proposed that the earth revolved around the sun decades before Galileo. To avoid being arrested, he fled to other European cities. He tried joining other religious groups such as the Calvinists in Geneva but was excommunicated from all of them. Bruno decided to come back to Rome and, in 1593, he was charged by the Inquisition for heresy. Seven years later, he was burned at the stake in this very location. To this day, he is a symbol of the Italian counter-culture and a martyr for freedom of thought. Various anti-authoritarian demonstrations are held in this square.<\/p>\n<p>Not too far off is the Theater of Pompey. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/place\/Largo+di+Torre+Argentina\/@41.8952749,12.4766142,3a,75y,301.55h,81.4t\/data=!3m8!1e1!3m6!1s-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB!2e4!3e11!6s%2F%2Flh6.googleusercontent.com%2F-2GV3uwHl3G8%2FWMPvG-oWvgI%2FAAAAAAAAAyM%2FqpLafv7dplQLsMxfDBnSquNQ8YVXWChvQCLIB%2Fw203-h100-k-no-pi-0-ya141.16286-ro-0-fo100%2F!7i8704!8i4352!4m12!1m6!3m5!1s0x132f6055a903cf7d:0x1808fc7ca708f2c4!2sCampo+de&apos;+Fiori!8m2!3d41.8955939!4d12.4721808!3m4!1s0x0:0xb57a25aa06db03cf!8m2!3d41.8954073!4d12.4768598!6m1!1e1\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Click here<\/u><\/a> to view the remains of the theater and the location where Julius Caesar was stabbed to death.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2051.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The palace on the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Farnese&lt;\/i&gt; is very important architecturally. It was built during the Renaissance by the nouveau riche Farnese Family. Pope Paul III, of the Farnese family, commissioned Michelangelo to design the top story. The building features a cornice, i.e. the jutting roofline, which can be seen on various other roman buildings. Moreover, the windows are framed with half-columns and topped with triangular pediments or semi-arches. The Pope made many of his speeches off of the building\u2019s balcony. Today, it is the home of the French Embassy.\" data-attachment-id=\"1470\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The palace on the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Farnese&lt;\/i&gt; is very important architecturally. It was built during the Renaissance by the nouveau riche Farnese Family. Pope Paul III, of the Farnese family, commissioned Michelangelo to design the top story. The building features a cornice, i.e. the jutting roofline, which can be seen on various other roman buildings. Moreover, the windows are framed with half-columns and topped with triangular pediments or semi-arches. The Pope made many of his speeches off of the building\u2019s balcony. Today, it is the home of the French Embassy.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2051\/2834225462.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The palace on the <i>Piazza Farnese<\/i> is very important architecturally. It was built during the Renaissance by the nouveau riche Farnese Family. Pope Paul III, of the Farnese family, commissioned Michelangelo to design the top story. The building features a cornice, i.e. the jutting roofline, which can be seen on various other roman buildings. Moreover, the windows are framed with half-columns and topped with triangular pediments or semi-arches. The Pope made many of his speeches off of the building\u2019s balcony. Today, it is the home of the French Embassy.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2056.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"&lt;i&gt;Pasquino&lt;\/i&gt; dates from the third century B.C. It once depicted King Menelaus holding his dying son\u2019s body. It functioned as a sort of community bulletin board for half a century. The plastic pane next to it continues to serve as a place to post political posters and strike announcements.\" data-attachment-id=\"1472\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"&lt;i&gt;Pasquino&lt;\/i&gt; dates from the third century B.C. It once depicted King Menelaus holding his dying son\u2019s body. It functioned as a sort of community bulletin board for half a century. The plastic pane next to it continues to serve as a place to post political posters and strike announcements.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2056\/854328062.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\"><i>Pasquino<\/i> dates from the third century B.C. It once depicted King Menelaus holding his dying son\u2019s body. It functioned as a sort of community bulletin board for half a century. The plastic pane next to it continues to serve as a place to post political posters and strike announcements.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2060.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Piazza Navona&lt;\/i&gt; was built around 80 A.D. by Emperor Domitian. As you can see form the oval shape, it initially served as a race track. It was transformed in the 17th century into what it is today by the popes as a form of piece offering to the citizens for all of the scandals that they had created.\" data-attachment-id=\"1473\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Piazza Navona&lt;\/i&gt; was built around 80 A.D. by Emperor Domitian. As you can see form the oval shape, it initially served as a race track. It was transformed in the 17th century into what it is today by the popes as a form of piece offering to the citizens for all of the scandals that they had created.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2060\/1758813684.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The <i>Piazza Navona<\/i> was built around 80 A.D. by Emperor Domitian. As you can see form the oval shape, it initially served as a race track. It was transformed in the 17th century into what it is today by the popes as a form of piece offering to the citizens for all of the scandals that they had created.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2063.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This is the first of three Baroque fountains on this square. It depicts an African wrestling a dolphin. During the time that it was built, Africans represented all that was exotic. Behind the fountain to the right is the &lt;i&gt;Palazzo Pamphilj&lt;\/i&gt;. Pope Innocent X came from the Pamphilj family and is the man responsible for the creation of this square.\" data-attachment-id=\"1474\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This is the first of three Baroque fountains on this square. It depicts an African wrestling a dolphin. During the time that it was built, Africans represented all that was exotic. Behind the fountain to the right is the &lt;i&gt;Palazzo Pamphilj&lt;\/i&gt;. Pope Innocent X came from the Pamphilj family and is the man responsible for the creation of this square.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2063\/699941229.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This is the first of three Baroque fountains on this square. It depicts an African wrestling a dolphin. During the time that it was built, Africans represented all that was exotic. Behind the fountain to the right is the <i>Palazzo Pamphilj<\/i>. Pope Innocent X came from the Pamphilj family and is the man responsible for the creation of this square.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2064.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Four Rivers Fountain&lt;\/i&gt; is centered on an obelisk. Ancient Romans used obelisks to show their triumph over the Egyptian empire. The obelisks were toppled when the city fell. Yet, the popes liked them and re-erected them with a cross on top. This fountain was built by Gian Lorenzo Bernini and depicts the four corners of the world. The man on the left is Daube representing the European continent. Danube is reaching for the Pamphilj coat of arms. To the right is Ganges, representing Asia.\" data-attachment-id=\"1475\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Four Rivers Fountain&lt;\/i&gt; is centered on an obelisk. Ancient Romans used obelisks to show their triumph over the Egyptian empire. The obelisks were toppled when the city fell. Yet, the popes liked them and re-erected them with a cross on top. This fountain was built by Gian Lorenzo Bernini and depicts the four corners of the world. The man on the left is Daube representing the European continent. Danube is reaching for the Pamphilj coat of arms. To the right is Ganges, representing Asia.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2064\/2259034684.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The <i>Four Rivers Fountain<\/i> is centered on an obelisk. Ancient Romans used obelisks to show their triumph over the Egyptian empire. The obelisks were toppled when the city fell. Yet, the popes liked them and re-erected them with a cross on top. This fountain was built by Gian Lorenzo Bernini and depicts the four corners of the world. The man on the left is Daube representing the European continent. Danube is reaching for the Pamphilj coat of arms. To the right is Ganges, representing Asia.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2067.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"On the other side of the fountain, on the left, you will see a palm tree and a man with his head covered by a cloth. This is Nile, representing Africa. The man is covered by the cloth to show the mystery of the river that they had not yet explored. Finally, the Rio de la Plata, representing the Americas, is looking up at the Church of Saint Agnes. Legend says that this final man is toppling back at the horror of the large church built by Borromini, as Bernini and Borromini were great Baroque artists and competitors at the time. Yet, this story cannot be true as the church was completed after the fountain. Moreover, while the church may look enormous, it is actually only the width of the four pillars framing the entry.\" data-attachment-id=\"1476\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"On the other side of the fountain, on the left, you will see a palm tree and a man with his head covered by a cloth. This is Nile, representing Africa. The man is covered by the cloth to show the mystery of the river that they had not yet explored. Finally, the Rio de la Plata, representing the Americas, is looking up at the Church of Saint Agnes. Legend says that this final man is toppling back at the horror of the large church built by Borromini, as Bernini and Borromini were great Baroque artists and competitors at the time. Yet, this story cannot be true as the church was completed after the fountain. Moreover, while the church may look enormous, it is actually only the width of the four pillars framing the entry.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2067\/1026923846.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">On the other side of the fountain, on the left, you will see a palm tree and a man with his head covered by a cloth. This is Nile, representing Africa. The man is covered by the cloth to show the mystery of the river that they had not yet explored. Finally, the Rio de la Plata, representing the Americas, is looking up at the Church of Saint Agnes. Legend says that this final man is toppling back at the horror of the large church built by Borromini, as Bernini and Borromini were great Baroque artists and competitors at the time. Yet, this story cannot be true as the church was completed after the fountain. Moreover, while the church may look enormous, it is actually only the width of the four pillars framing the entry.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2068.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The final fountain on the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Navona&lt;\/i&gt; is of Neptune slaying an octopus.\" data-attachment-id=\"1477\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The final fountain on the &lt;i&gt;Piazza Navona&lt;\/i&gt; is of Neptune slaying an octopus.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2068\/1104078242.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The final fountain on the <i>Piazza Navona<\/i> is of Neptune slaying an octopus.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2069.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Palazzo Madama&lt;\/i&gt; is named after Madame Margherita, who married into the Medici Family. Two Medici boys grew up in this house to later become popes during Michelangelo\u2019s time. It now houses the Italian Senate.\" data-attachment-id=\"1478\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The &lt;i&gt;Palazzo Madama&lt;\/i&gt; is named after Madame Margherita, who married into the Medici Family. Two Medici boys grew up in this house to later become popes during Michelangelo\u2019s time. It now houses the Italian Senate.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2069\/1812643777.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The <i>Palazzo Madama<\/i> is named after Madame Margherita, who married into the Medici Family. Two Medici boys grew up in this house to later become popes during Michelangelo\u2019s time. It now houses the Italian Senate.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2072.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Church of San Luigi dei Fancesi is the French national church in Rome. This is made evident by the statue of Charlemagne on the left and the fire-breathing reptile underneath, a symbol of Fran\u00e7ois I. Additionally, you will notice a coat of arms with three fleur-de-lys at the top of the building.\" data-attachment-id=\"1480\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Church of San Luigi dei Fancesi is the French national church in Rome. This is made evident by the statue of Charlemagne on the left and the fire-breathing reptile underneath, a symbol of Fran\u00e7ois I. Additionally, you will notice a coat of arms with three fleur-de-lys at the top of the building.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2072\/3413776040.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Church of San Luigi dei Fancesi is the French national church in Rome. This is made evident by the statue of Charlemagne on the left and the fire-breathing reptile underneath, a symbol of Fran\u00e7ois I. Additionally, you will notice a coat of arms with three fleur-de-lys at the top of the building.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2070.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Inside the church is the Contarelli Chapel, which houses three of Michelangelo Merisi da Caravaggio\u2019s paintings. They are centered on the theme of Saint Mathew.\" data-attachment-id=\"1479\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Inside the church is the Contarelli Chapel, which houses three of Michelangelo Merisi da Caravaggio\u2019s paintings. They are centered on the theme of Saint Mathew.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2070\/3253066465.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Inside the church is the Contarelli Chapel, which houses three of Michelangelo Merisi da Caravaggio\u2019s paintings. They are centered on the theme of Saint Mathew.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2095.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Obelisks were used by the Egyptian rulers as monuments to the sun god Ra. They are said to symbolize a sun ray and the heavenly energy that comes with it. Roman emperors liked the idea and brought them over from Egypt once they triumphed over the empire. Doing so was a rather challenging task as the obelisks are carved out of a single piece of stone by the Egyptians which is then toppled by the Romans and brought over in ships. This obelisk dates back to the sixth century and is the only one that isn\u2019t topped with a cross as the pope\u2019s had a practice of doing. Also, this obelisk still serves as a sundial.\n&lt;p&gt;The building behind the obelisk is Parliament and was designed by Bernini.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1488\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Obelisks were used by the Egyptian rulers as monuments to the sun god Ra. They are said to symbolize a sun ray and the heavenly energy that comes with it. Roman emperors liked the idea and brought them over from Egypt once they triumphed over the empire. Doing so was a rather challenging task as the obelisks are carved out of a single piece of stone by the Egyptians which is then toppled by the Romans and brought over in ships. This obelisk dates back to the sixth century and is the only one that isn\u2019t topped with a cross as the pope\u2019s had a practice of doing. Also, this obelisk still serves as a sundial.\n&lt;p&gt;The building behind the obelisk is Parliament and was designed by Bernini.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2095\/670139812.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Obelisks were used by the Egyptian rulers as monuments to the sun god Ra. They are said to symbolize a sun ray and the heavenly energy that comes with it. Roman emperors liked the idea and brought them over from Egypt once they triumphed over the empire. Doing so was a rather challenging task as the obelisks are carved out of a single piece of stone by the Egyptians which is then toppled by the Romans and brought over in ships. This obelisk dates back to the sixth century and is the only one that isn\u2019t topped with a cross as the pope\u2019s had a practice of doing. Also, this obelisk still serves as a sundial.\n<p>The building behind the obelisk is Parliament and was designed by Bernini.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2098.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This column dates from the second century and tells a story of Emperor Marcus Aurelis, who battled the barbarians in 170 A.D. It is made of 28 cylinder and stands 97 feet tall. It&#039;s hollow inside and has stairs leading all the way to the top. At the top used to stand a statue of the Emperor but it was replaced in after Rome&#039;s fall by the Christian Paul.\" data-attachment-id=\"1489\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This column dates from the second century and tells a story of Emperor Marcus Aurelis, who battled the barbarians in 170 A.D. It is made of 28 cylinder and stands 97 feet tall. It&#039;s hollow inside and has stairs leading all the way to the top. At the top used to stand a statue of the Emperor but it was replaced in after Rome&#039;s fall by the Christian Paul.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2098\/422574653.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This column dates from the second century and tells a story of Emperor Marcus Aurelis, who battled the barbarians in 170 A.D. It is made of 28 cylinder and stands 97 feet tall. It's hollow inside and has stairs leading all the way to the top. At the top used to stand a statue of the Emperor but it was replaced in after Rome's fall by the Christian Paul.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2101.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Fontana di Trevi - While a very famous fountain in Rome, it was built less than 250 years ago. The fountain took thirty years to build after Pope Clement XII approved the designs by Nicola Salvi. Neptune is flanked by two horses, who represent the two states of the sea. The horse on the left is wild, like the sea during a storm while the horse on the right is calm. The two female sculptures in the cutouts symbolize abundance (left) and health (right).\" data-attachment-id=\"1490\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Fontana di Trevi - While a very famous fountain in Rome, it was built less than 250 years ago. The fountain took thirty years to build after Pope Clement XII approved the designs by Nicola Salvi. Neptune is flanked by two horses, who represent the two states of the sea. The horse on the left is wild, like the sea during a storm while the horse on the right is calm. The two female sculptures in the cutouts symbolize abundance (left) and health (right).\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2101\/3069445492.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Fontana di Trevi - While a very famous fountain in Rome, it was built less than 250 years ago. The fountain took thirty years to build after Pope Clement XII approved the designs by Nicola Salvi. Neptune is flanked by two horses, who represent the two states of the sea. The horse on the left is wild, like the sea during a storm while the horse on the right is calm. The two female sculptures in the cutouts symbolize abundance (left) and health (right).<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2106.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Immaculate Conception Column is topped by a statue of Mary, which may be a bit hard to discern against the deep blue sky. Regardless, every year on December 8th, the Pope climbs a firetruck\u2019s ladder and places fresh flowers high on the statue. That kicks off the roman Christmas Season.\n&lt;p&gt;To the right of the column, you can just barely see the corner of the Spanish Embassy to the Vatican City. Because the Vatican is so small, Rome has twice the number on embassies. Each country has one for Rome and one for the Vatican.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1491\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Immaculate Conception Column is topped by a statue of Mary, which may be a bit hard to discern against the deep blue sky. Regardless, every year on December 8th, the Pope climbs a firetruck\u2019s ladder and places fresh flowers high on the statue. That kicks off the roman Christmas Season.\n&lt;p&gt;To the right of the column, you can just barely see the corner of the Spanish Embassy to the Vatican City. Because the Vatican is so small, Rome has twice the number on embassies. Each country has one for Rome and one for the Vatican.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2106\/434791973.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Immaculate Conception Column is topped by a statue of Mary, which may be a bit hard to discern against the deep blue sky. Regardless, every year on December 8th, the Pope climbs a firetruck\u2019s ladder and places fresh flowers high on the statue. That kicks off the roman Christmas Season.\n<p>To the right of the column, you can just barely see the corner of the Spanish Embassy to the Vatican City. Because the Vatican is so small, Rome has twice the number on embassies. Each country has one for Rome and one for the Vatican.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2108.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Sinking Boat Fountain was built by either Gian Lorenzo Bernini or his father, Pietro Bernini. Because the water pressure from the Aqua Vergine aqueduct is so low, it cannot shoot up and was instead designed to fill the boat with water. The fountain rest at the base of the Spanish Steps.\" data-attachment-id=\"1492\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Sinking Boat Fountain was built by either Gian Lorenzo Bernini or his father, Pietro Bernini. Because the water pressure from the Aqua Vergine aqueduct is so low, it cannot shoot up and was instead designed to fill the boat with water. The fountain rest at the base of the Spanish Steps.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2108\/964480142.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Sinking Boat Fountain was built by either Gian Lorenzo Bernini or his father, Pietro Bernini. Because the water pressure from the Aqua Vergine aqueduct is so low, it cannot shoot up and was instead designed to fill the boat with water. The fountain rest at the base of the Spanish Steps.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2111.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Spanish Steps were built about 300 years ago. They are named so due to the proximity of the Spanish Embassy, mentioned before. Most notably, two British poets lived nearby: John Keats (in the house numbered 26) and Lord Byron (in the house numbered 66).\" data-attachment-id=\"1493\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Spanish Steps were built about 300 years ago. They are named so due to the proximity of the Spanish Embassy, mentioned before. Most notably, two British poets lived nearby: John Keats (in the house numbered 26) and Lord Byron (in the house numbered 66).\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2111\/2492705198.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Spanish Steps were built about 300 years ago. They are named so due to the proximity of the Spanish Embassy, mentioned before. Most notably, two British poets lived nearby: John Keats (in the house numbered 26) and Lord Byron (in the house numbered 66).<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2196.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This circular building was first built in 139 AD as a mausoleum of Hadrian. It was later turned into a fortification by Emperor Aurelian. It had various uses after that as a prison and then a papal residence. In 1527, Pope Clement VII used it as a refuge. Finally, Pope Gregory I led a procession during the plague to ask mercy. The participants of the procession saw an angel appear over the building and signified an end to the city\u2019s misfortune. Since then, it has been known as Castel Sant&#039;Angelo. Originally the building was crowned by a large statue of the emperor, which was later replaced by several different statues of the Archangel that appeared.\" data-attachment-id=\"1446\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This circular building was first built in 139 AD as a mausoleum of Hadrian. It was later turned into a fortification by Emperor Aurelian. It had various uses after that as a prison and then a papal residence. In 1527, Pope Clement VII used it as a refuge. Finally, Pope Gregory I led a procession during the plague to ask mercy. The participants of the procession saw an angel appear over the building and signified an end to the city\u2019s misfortune. Since then, it has been known as Castel Sant&#039;Angelo. Originally the building was crowned by a large statue of the emperor, which was later replaced by several different statues of the Archangel that appeared.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2196\/1208094319.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This circular building was first built in 139 AD as a mausoleum of Hadrian. It was later turned into a fortification by Emperor Aurelian. It had various uses after that as a prison and then a papal residence. In 1527, Pope Clement VII used it as a refuge. Finally, Pope Gregory I led a procession during the plague to ask mercy. The participants of the procession saw an angel appear over the building and signified an end to the city\u2019s misfortune. Since then, it has been known as Castel Sant'Angelo. Originally the building was crowned by a large statue of the emperor, which was later replaced by several different statues of the Archangel that appeared.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2189.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Painting inside the Castel Sant&#039;Angelo\" data-attachment-id=\"1445\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Painting inside the Castel Sant&#039;Angelo\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2189\/3007688729.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Painting inside the Castel Sant'Angelo<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2175.jpg\" data-attachment-id=\"1442\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2175\/4105269757.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2182.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Description: &lt;i&gt;A statue of the angel was placed on top of the building. It was originally a wooden sculpture, which has not survived; a second angel, in marble, was destroyed in 1397 during a siege and replaced in 1453 with a marble angel with bronze wings. This angel was destroyed by a lightning bolt in 1497, which caused a powder store in the castle to explode, and was then replaced by a gilt bronze sculpture that was melted down in 1527 to make cannons. In the 16th century a marble statue with bronze wing was made by Montelupo, and the bronze angel we see today was made in 1753 by Peter Anton Van Verschaffelt.&lt;\/i&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1444\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Description: &lt;i&gt;A statue of the angel was placed on top of the building. It was originally a wooden sculpture, which has not survived; a second angel, in marble, was destroyed in 1397 during a siege and replaced in 1453 with a marble angel with bronze wings. This angel was destroyed by a lightning bolt in 1497, which caused a powder store in the castle to explode, and was then replaced by a gilt bronze sculpture that was melted down in 1527 to make cannons. In the 16th century a marble statue with bronze wing was made by Montelupo, and the bronze angel we see today was made in 1753 by Peter Anton Van Verschaffelt.&lt;\/i&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2182\/1274332679.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Description: <i>A statue of the angel was placed on top of the building. It was originally a wooden sculpture, which has not survived; a second angel, in marble, was destroyed in 1397 during a siege and replaced in 1453 with a marble angel with bronze wings. This angel was destroyed by a lightning bolt in 1497, which caused a powder store in the castle to explode, and was then replaced by a gilt bronze sculpture that was melted down in 1527 to make cannons. In the 16th century a marble statue with bronze wing was made by Montelupo, and the bronze angel we see today was made in 1753 by Peter Anton Van Verschaffelt.<\/i><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2176.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"View of St. Peter&#039;s Basilica from the roof of Castel Sant&#039;Angelo\" data-attachment-id=\"1443\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"View of St. Peter&#039;s Basilica from the roof of Castel Sant&#039;Angelo\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2176\/3756334166.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">View of St. Peter's Basilica from the roof of Castel Sant'Angelo<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><\/div>\r\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"Pantheon\"><style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-1496 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox-free fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-small fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-1496\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:false},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2075.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Pantheon is dedicated to all (pan-) gods (-theos). It used to stand tall on this square and was approached by steps. With the movement of sediment, the Pantheon is sinking. This can be seen in the disappearing steps of the fountain. The fountain was built in the 18th century, more than 1700 years after the Pantheon, which is why it has not yet sunk to that level.\" data-attachment-id=\"1481\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Pantheon is dedicated to all (pan-) gods (-theos). It used to stand tall on this square and was approached by steps. With the movement of sediment, the Pantheon is sinking. This can be seen in the disappearing steps of the fountain. The fountain was built in the 18th century, more than 1700 years after the Pantheon, which is why it has not yet sunk to that level.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2075\/1749408146.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Pantheon is dedicated to all (pan-) gods (-theos). It used to stand tall on this square and was approached by steps. With the movement of sediment, the Pantheon is sinking. This can be seen in the disappearing steps of the fountain. The fountain was built in the 18th century, more than 1700 years after the Pantheon, which is why it has not yet sunk to that level.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2079.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In order to support the dome, the walls had to be 20 feet thick and could not have windows. For this reason, the only source of light is the opening in the middle of the dome measuring 30 feet wide. The dome include a pattern of square cut-outs to reduce the weight while keeping the strength of the architecture. The floor is slanted outwards and has holes in the ground to help with the draining when it rains in the Pantheon.\" data-attachment-id=\"1482\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"In order to support the dome, the walls had to be 20 feet thick and could not have windows. For this reason, the only source of light is the opening in the middle of the dome measuring 30 feet wide. The dome include a pattern of square cut-outs to reduce the weight while keeping the strength of the architecture. The floor is slanted outwards and 